Call-site examples (use these as a guide for constructing the target call):
<xref>
public void testGetMaxMiddleIndex() {
        TimePeriodValues s = new TimePeriodValues("Test");
        assertEquals(-1, s.getMaxMiddleIndex());
        s.add(new SimpleTimePeriod(100L, 200L), 1.0);
        assertEquals(0, s.getMaxMiddleIndex());
        s.add(new SimpleTimePeriod(300L, 400L), 2.0);
        assertEquals(1, s.getMaxMiddleIndex());
        s.add(new SimpleTimePeriod(0L, 50L), 3.0);
        assertEquals(1, s.getMaxMiddleIndex());
        s.add(new SimpleTimePeriod(150L, 200L), 4.0);
        assertEquals(1, s.getMaxMiddleIndex());
    }
</xref>
STATE COUPLING: these members of the same class share the listed field(s) with `getMaxMiddleIndex` but neither calls the other — state one of them writes is what the other reports. A defect the patch leaves (or introduces) in that shared state stays observable through these members even when `getMaxMiddleIndex`'s own output looks right, so a strong harness CHECKS THEY AGREE: compare what a reader reports against what the writer/constructor established.
  - `private void updateBounds(TimePeriod period, int index)` (shared field(s): maxMiddleIndex)
      doc: Update the index values for the maximum and minimum bounds. @param period  the time period. @param index  the index of the time period.
  - `private void recalculateBounds()` (shared field(s): maxMiddleIndex)
      doc: Recalculates the bounds for the collection of items.
  - `public int hashCode()` (shared field(s): maxMiddleIndex)
      doc: Returns a hash code value for the object. @return The hashcode

